WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.250
Follow an oceanic plate toward a continent.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.670
The plate bends downward into a trench, which traps sediment.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.550
Material scraped from the descending plate contributes to an accretionary prism.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.310
Between the trench and the main mountain front lies the forearc

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.350
basin. These features occupy specific positions:

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.610
the trench is not the foreland basin, and the prism is

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.050
not the volcanic arc.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.780
Magma rises into the continent, crystallizing at depth or erupting at

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.960
the surface. Compression can also fold and thrust the crust, making

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.280
it thicker. Behind the main mountain belt, thrust sheets load the

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.840
plate and bend it downward into a foreland basin.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.460
Sediment eroded from the rising mountains fills that depression.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.140
Some margins have weak compression and lack this complete thrust-and-foreland arrangement.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.530
The overriding plate can shorten or extend.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.250
If the continent moves toward the slab relative to the asthenosphere,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.610
pushing against the slab promotes compression.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.070
If the overriding plate does not advance toward it, or moves

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.950
away, slab retreat can stretch the upper plate.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.570
That is why extension can occur above a subduction zone even

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.290
though the two plates converge overall.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.600
Continental crust resists deep subduction because of its buoyancy.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.420
Collision underthrusts one continent beneath another, stacks crust, and raises mountains

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.580
or a plateau. Thrust loads create adjacent basins.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.940
If the thickened crust becomes sufficiently hot and weak, it can

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.080
spread sideways under its own weight, producing local normal faults.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.520
Collision effects can also extend far into the continental interior.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.150
The Devonian Catskill system records an ancient collision and foreland basin

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.250
east of today's continental interior.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.570
Thrust loading depressed the crust, while mountains supplied sediment westward.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:46.410
Coarser and thicker deposits near the eastern source preserve that relationship.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.470
Later uplift and erosion exposed the record.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.370
Use sediment distribution together with deformation to reconstruct the mountains and

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.270
basin as one evolving system.
